# Two separate reasons, because only one of them used to be true.
#
# 1. Dependencies do not belong in git. Uncontroversial.
#
# 2. It keeps a stray `npm install` out of the link checker's input set — but
#    ONLY because the rot detector's input is now `.` rather than the glob
#    './**/*.md'. lychee honours .gitignore for a DIRECTORY input and ignores
#    it for an explicit glob. Measured on a checkout with node_modules
#    present: `.` → 14 links, exit 0; './**/*.md' → 59 links, exit 2 on a
#    dependency's own broken link, with this file in place and unchanged.
#    Removing this file entirely made no difference to the glob run.
#
#    So while the workflow passed a glob, the previous version of this comment
#    described a protection that did not operate. The workflow was changed to
#    `.` and the claim is now load-bearing: if the input ever goes back to a
#    glob, this stops working again and this comment becomes wrong again.
#
# The primary defence remains structural rather than this file: the drift
# check installs its one dependency into RUNNER_TEMP, never the workspace, so
# no node_modules exists in the checkout for either input to reach — this
# workflow's first real run failed on `node_modules/yaml/docs/CONTRIBUTING.md`
# and an npmjs.com 403, neither of which is this repository's content. The
# required gate separately uses `git ls-files`, which an untracked path cannot
# enter at all.
node_modules/
